## db Types & Data Structures
##
## Part of the Alya Language package ecosystem (https://github.com/alya-lang).
##
## Abstraction-layer models shared by every database driver. Drivers keep
## their own twins of these shapes (e.g. `sqlite::DbParam`) and convert at
## the adapter boundary; this package never imports a driver, so a new
## driver (postgres, mysql, ...) plugs in without revising anything here.

## Bound parameter kinds for `?` placeholders.
##
## The union every driver must accept. Drivers needing richer types map
## them through these five (timestamps as text, booleans as int, ...).
pub enum ParamKind
    Int = 1
    Float = 2
    Text = 3
    Null = 4
    Blob = 5
end

## A single explicitly-typed bound parameter.
##
## Static constructor types (`v: int`, `v: float`, ...) keep each payload
## exact all the way into the driver bind call; drivers dispatch on `kind`,
## never by guessing the dynamic value type.
##
## ### Fields
## - `kind`: One of `ParamKind` (1=int, 2=float, 3=text, 4=null, 5=blob).
## - `ival`: Integer payload for `ParamKind.Int`.
## - `fval`: Float payload for `ParamKind.Float`.
## - `sval`: Text payload for `ParamKind.Text`.
## - `bval`: Byte array payload (`[0..255]`) for `ParamKind.Blob`.
pub struct Param
    kind: int
    ival: int
    fval: float
    sval: string
    bval
end

## Creates an integer bound parameter.
##
## ### Parameters
## - `v`: Integer value to bind.
##
## ### Returns
## A `Param` with `ParamKind.Int`.
pub function param_int(v: int) -> Param
    return Param { kind: ParamKind.Int, ival: v, fval: 0.0, sval: "", bval: [] }
end

## Creates a float bound parameter.
##
## The text rendering is cached into `sval` at construction, where `v` is
## still statically proven: after multi-hop travel through untyped params
## and struct fields, `fval` comes back with corrupted bits on current
## compilers, while strings travel intact. Drivers read floats from `sval`.
##
## ### Parameters
## - `v`: Float value to bind.
##
## ### Returns
## A `Param` with `ParamKind.Float`.
pub function param_float(v: float) -> Param
    return Param { kind: ParamKind.Float, ival: 0, fval: v, sval: str(v), bval: [] }
end

## Creates a text bound parameter.
##
## ### Parameters
## - `v`: Text value to bind (quotes need no escaping).
##
## ### Returns
## A `Param` with `ParamKind.Text`.
pub function param_text(v: string) -> Param
    return Param { kind: ParamKind.Text, ival: 0, fval: 0.0, sval: v, bval: [] }
end

## Creates a null bound parameter.
##
## ### Returns
## A `Param` with `ParamKind.Null`.
pub function param_null() -> Param
    return Param { kind: ParamKind.Null, ival: 0, fval: 0.0, sval: "", bval: [] }
end

## Creates a blob bound parameter.
##
## ### Parameters
## - `v`: Byte array (`[0..255]`) to bind; may be empty.
##
## ### Returns
## A `Param` with `ParamKind.Blob`.
pub function param_blob(v) -> Param
    return Param { kind: ParamKind.Blob, ival: 0, fval: 0.0, sval: "", bval: v }
end

## Structured database error payload.
##
## Drivers throw this (never raw strings) so callers can match on `code`
## inside `catch` blocks. Thrown values keep their fields; see Chapter 10.
##
## ### Fields
## - `code`: Driver-defined code (`1` generic, `2` usage/mismatch, others per driver).
## - `message`: Human-readable description.
pub struct DbError
    code: int
    message: string
end

## Creates a `DbError` payload for throwing.
##
## ### Parameters
## - `code`: Driver-defined error code integer.
## - `message`: Human-readable description string.
##
## ### Returns
## A `DbError` ready to `throw`.
pub function db_error(code: int, message: string) -> DbError
    return DbError { code: code, message: message }
end

## A database driver: a name plus four function hooks.
##
## Drivers are plain structs holding functions (called via a local binding,
## as in `Lib/http`'s router), so registering a new driver never touches
## this package. Hook contracts:
## - `open_fn(path: string)` returns a driver-defined handle (struct, map...).
## - `close_fn(handle)` returns an int result code (`0` on success).
## - `execute_fn(handle, sql: string, params: array)` returns the affected
##   row count integer.
## - `query_fn(handle, sql: string, params: array)` returns an array of row
##   maps (possibly empty).
## Every hook throws `DbError` on failure. `params` holds `Param` entries,
## one per `?` placeholder in order (possibly empty).
pub struct Driver
    name: string
    open_fn
    close_fn
    execute_fn
    query_fn
end

## An open database connection: a driver plus its native handle.
##
## ### Fields
## - `driver`: Owning `Driver`; facade calls dispatch through its hooks.
## - `handle`: Driver-defined native handle (kept opaque by this package).
## - `path`: Connection target string passed to `open_fn`.
## - `is_open`: `1` while open, `0` after close.
## - `pool_id`: Pool checkout tag (`0` = unpooled; set by `ConnPool.acquire`).
pub struct Conn
    driver
    handle
    path: string
    is_open: int
    pool_id: int
end

## A bounded pool of reusable connections with explicit context.
##
## Unlike generic callback pools, every operation carries its driver and
## path explicitly, so no closures are needed (Alya has no capturing
## closures yet). Idle connections are reused in LIFO order; broken ones
## are dropped via `invalidate`.
##
## ### Fields
## - `driver`: Driver used to open pooled connections.
## - `path`: Connection target string passed to `open_fn`.
## - `max_size`: Maximum live connections (idle + active); `0` rejects.
## - `idle`: Array of idle `Conn` available for reuse.
## - `active`: Array of checked-out `Conn`.
## - `next_id`: Counter assigning `pool_id` tags.
## - `closed`: `1` after `close`, `0` otherwise.
pub struct ConnPool
    driver
    path: string
    max_size: int
    idle
    active
    next_id: int
    closed: int
end
